  <section>
    <h2>What are commissions in a PNR?</h2>
    <p>A <strong>commission</strong> is the percentage or fixed amount an airline, operator, or consolidator pays your agency for selling their service. In an Amadeus PNR, this information can appear in several ways depending on the GDS and issuance type.</p>

    <p>Some of the most common fields are:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong><code>FM</code></strong> (Form of Payment / Commission): shows the commission percentage assigned to the booking.</li>
      <li><strong><code>FP</code></strong> (Form of Payment): indicates payment method and sometimes breaks down amounts.</li>
      <li><strong><code>FS</code></strong> or <code>FE</code>: fields related to fare rules and special fares.</li>
      <li><strong>Fare lines:</strong> contain the base price, taxes, and fees of the ticket.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The key is understanding that the PNR contains the <strong>cost</strong> of the service, but not the <strong>selling price</strong>. The final price is defined by your agency applying the appropriate markup.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Basic formula for calculating commissions</h2>
    <p>The most common formula for calculating the final selling price is:</p>
    <pre><code>Final price = (Net price + Taxes) × (1 + Desired markup)</code></pre>
    <p>Where:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Net price:</strong> what the agency pays the operator or airline.</li>
      <li><strong>Taxes:</strong> mandatory airport taxes, fees, and charges.</li>
      <li><strong>Markup:</strong> the commercial margin the agency wants to earn.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For example, if a flight has a net price of USD 800, taxes of USD 200, and you want a 10% markup, the calculation would be:</p>
    <pre><code>(800 + 200) × 1.10 = USD 1,100</code></pre>
    <p>The commission you receive from the airline (for example, 7%) is calculated on the net price:</p>
    <pre><code>800 × 0.07 = USD 56</code></pre>
    <p>This means your total profit is the sum of the applied markup plus the commission received, unless you are working on a net model.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How to read commission fields in the PNR</h2>
    <p>Suppose you have this line in a PNR:</p>
    <pre><code>FM CASH 7.00%</code></pre>
    <p>This indicates the commission is 7% of the ticket value. If the base fare is USD 500, the commission would be USD 35.</p>

    <p>Another common example:</p>
    <pre><code>FP CASH/XXX 1200.00</code></pre>
    <p>Here, <code>1200.00</code> represents the total charged amount, which may include base fare plus taxes. To know how much is commission, you first need to identify the net price and then apply the corresponding percentage.</p>

    <h3>Markup vs. commission?</h3>
    <p>Many agencies confuse markup with commission. <strong>Commission</strong> is what the supplier pays you. <strong>Markup</strong> is what you add for your service. Both are part of your profitability, but they are calculated differently and should be recorded separately.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Common mistakes when calculating commissions</h2>
    <p>These mistakes happen in agencies of all sizes. Know them to avoid them:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Calculating commission on the total including taxes:</strong> most airlines pay commission only on the base fare, not on taxes.</li>
      <li><strong>Confusing net price with selling price:</strong> selling at cost without markup means working for free.</li>
      <li><strong>Not updating percentages:</strong> commissions change. If you use an outdated table, you lose money.</li>
      <li><strong>Showing internal calculations to the client:</strong> sending the PNR with <code>FM</code> or <code>RM</code> lines exposes your business.</li>
      <li><strong>Forgetting local taxes:</strong> each destination has additional fees that impact the final price.</li>
    </ul>

